# Test fixtures are vendored from upstream and compared byte for byte, so they
# have to check out with exactly the bytes they were vendored with. Several of
# them are CRLF, because ALE and EDL are CRLF formats, so `text eol=lf` is the
# wrong rule: it leaves those bytes alone on checkout but normalises them away
# on the next check-in, which both reports every one of them as modified in a
# clean tree and quietly strips the CRs out of the next commit that touches
# them. Marking them as not-text means git never converts them either way.
*.ale -text
*.edl -text
*.otio -text
*.tsv -text

# Structured storage containers, which git must never touch.
*.aaf binary

# What upstream's AAF adapter wrote for those containers, compared byte for
# byte, so for the same reason as the fixtures above git must not convert them.
crates/otio-aaf/tests/data/*.json -text

# Vendored XML fixtures, a couple of which upstream wrote with CRLF. `-text`
# stops git converting them in either direction, so every platform gets the
# bytes they were vendored with.
crates/otio-fcp7/tests/data/*.xml -text
crates/otio-fcpx/tests/data/*.fcpxml -text

# Generated SDKs, which a test regenerates and compares against what is
# committed. The generator writes LF, so a checkout that converts them to CRLF
# reports every generated file as drifted; that is how the Windows job first
# failed. `text eol=lf` keeps the repository's LF and hands out LF everywhere.
sdk/** text eol=lf

# The TypeScript SDK's generated files, for the same reason. They were written
# by a generator of their own until the TypeScript backend moved into
# `otio-sdk-gen`, so they were only ever checked on Linux and this rule was
# not needed; the workspace drift test now checks them on Windows too.
crates/otio-wasm/ts/src/generated/** text eol=lf
crates/otio-wasm/src/layout.rs text eol=lf

# The CI selector is run by bash on a Linux runner, where a CRLF line ending
# fails the whole script with `\r: command not found`.
.github/ci/*.sh text eol=lf
